=== CURRENT WORK ===

Touch panels, the guided setup: BUILT and verified on Pi A with a virtual panel (8 cases end to end, including a real tap through the compositor, and the web dialog in a browser): mapping by the four quarters, calibration by five points, the totem's veil (lit quarter / circle, no
text), "Try it", nothing stored before "Yes, keep it". NOT done: the real panel of Pi A has not been set up by a person yet (needs the user's finger: Display page -> "Touch doesn't match? Fix it"); on Pi A the compositor's `LANG=C` is a volatile drop-in (the unit change is in
debian/unitotem-viewer.unitotem-compositor.service, shipped by the next deb), so a reboot of Pi A loses it and a calibration with decimals would not apply until then.

=== OPEN (history: docs/TODO-archive.md; what each thing is: CLAUDE.md) ===

--- To build (mine), decided with the user on 2026-11-01; order: the simplest first, each closed for good ---
1. (nothing left to build: GPIO display power is built, see the line under 'Needs hardware'.)

--- Later, asked for by the user through unitotem-system (NOT started; do not start it before the user says) ---
* Boot gap of about 7 s between Plymouth and the first frame of the boot splash, with the kernel console text on screen (measured on a Pi by unitotem-system: `plymouth deactivate` in
  the compositor's ExecStartPre at ~12.17 s, labwc execs at ~12.41 s, `unitotem-splash` (bootsplash.py) paints its first frame at ~19.7 s; tty1 is a text console meanwhile). A desktop avoids it because
  the display manager puts the VT in graphics mode and ends Plymouth with `plymouth quit --retain-splash`, so the last frame stays. To try by hand on the Pi first, no reboot needed: (1) put tty1 in
  graphics mode (KD_GRAPHICS) as soon as labwc starts so the kernel stops drawing text; (2) replace `pkill -KILL plymouthd` + `cat /dev/zero > /dev/fb0` in the compositor's ExecStartPost with
  `plymouth quit --retain-splash`; (3) have labwc paint the splash background at once, and/or give the splash a normal CPUWeight and a faster start. The unit files are in the unitotem-viewer deb.
  Kernel logs stay on the journal/serial: the user does not want them disabled.

--- Waiting for the user (decisions and things only they can see) ---
* Wi-Fi roaming: Pi A's profile was unlocked and its DNS repaired (2026-11-01), but wpa_supplicant still picks a -66 dBm AP over a -38 dBm one of the same network and never roams (no signal monitoring on the Pi 4): a node-side "prefer the strongest access point" (event-driven, reactivate on a stronger AP) is proposed to the user, not built.
* Long press on the real panel: no context menu should appear (Pi A, dev code mounted; needs the user's finger).
* H.264/AAC: the build ships the proprietary codecs (user rule); the patent/licensing question moves to whoever distributes the image (VM-TESTING.md 8b), not a technical decision.

--- Waiting for unitotem-system (image) or for the next deb set ---
* dev19 debs (unitotem-system said on 2026-11-01 that image 2026.10.43 = dev18 + a Wi-Fi reconnect fix is on all three boards and that new sets can be built): everything since dev18 (touch name fix, inert pages, screen-off fix, names hardening, lockdown, developer tools, language per window, locale paks, profile
  preferences, labwc rc.xml without default keybinds, hotspot fix). The user wants them AFTER the 'to build' list (2026-11-01); cefpython is unchanged.
* `/etc/chromium.d/50-unitotem-accel` is still in the image and configures a browser that is not installed (the viewer sets its switches itself).
* Identity merge at OTA (`migrate-etc-overlay`): accounts and root's password across a slot switch, verify with a real A/B cycle; the browser profile (cookies) across a slot switch.
* A session bus for root so GUI applications reach the notification daemon; `unitotem-boot-orientation` installed on boards (Plymouth turned); the new `rc.xml` (Alt+F4 etc.).
* Mixed versions (old viewer with a new manager and the reverse) and rollback to the previous slot with a turned screen were reasoned, never run.

--- Needs hardware or a setup I do not have ---
* GPIO display power (relay or button line, `system/screens/gpio.py`, Display page): unit-tested against a fake libgpiod only; never run on a real line (wiring is the installer's) nor against Debian's python3-libgpiod 2.x on a board.
* CEC (no adapter), serial power commands (no port), Wake-on-LAN of a monitor with a NIC, DDC writes on monitors other than the LG and the Realtek board.
* A client (remote) viewer's pixels, the approval click path in the UI, behind a firewall that blocks 4443, multi-GB transfers, and an adoption/unpair between two real nodes with window settings and layouts, and the push of files to a client (both unit-tested only; the boards are the system session's and an adoption empties the client).
* Camera and microphone: `getUserMedia` answers `NotFoundError` on the boards (they have neither); a real capture was never seen.
* Real playback checks left in VM-TESTING.md section 7; per-asset volume and per-window audio output have been checked on a Pi with the capture card, not on a remote viewer.

--- Closed, so nobody asks again ---
Decided 2026-11-01: TCP 4443 stays open on the image; the upstream pull requests for CEF and cefpython are NOT made now (the patches and docs/cef-wayland/pr-*.md stay ready); dev19 debs are built after the 'to build' list.
Superseded entries of the old log: the welcome screen on a genuine first boot, the last screen going unnoticed, the scrollbar on the welcome page, the codecs, the interim per-window ticker store
(`WindowLayout.ticker` is the store), Updates page against the real updater (checked on the boards), timers and screens, VNC, suspend, PAM accounts, first access, notification center,
Wayland, rotation by the compositor, cookies, the keyboard, the network drops (2026-10-31), the lean CEF build (measured, not worth it: docs/cef-lean-build.md).

